    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to requiring\ncurriculum related to human trafficking awareness to be included in alcohol\ntraining awareness programs",
    "basePrintNo": "S7360",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7360-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 5,
       "fullName": "Jim Gaughran",
       "imgName": "1042_Jim_Gaughran.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1222,
       "sessionMemberId": 1807,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "GAUGHRAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§18 & 17, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7360\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nrequiring curriculum related to human trafficking awareness to be\nincluded in alcohol training awareness programs\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo implement a requirement that human trafficking awareness trainings be\nincluded in all SLA certified Alcohol Training Awareness Program\n\n(ATAP) bartending courses that are recognized in New York State.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: amends Subdivision 10 of section 18 of the alcoholic beverage\ncontrol law by adding a requirement that all alcohol training awareness\nprograms that the chairman develops include training related to human\ntrafficking awareness, to be developed in conjunction with, and approved\nby, the New York state interagency task force on human trafficking\n\nSection 2: amends Subdivision 12 of section 17 of the alcoholic beverage\ncontrol law by adding a requirement that all alcohol training awareness\nprograms that the authority develops include training related to human\ntrafficking awareness, to be developed in conjunction with, and approved\nby, the New York state interagency task force on human trafficking.\n\nSection 3: Sets effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe SLA currently recognizes 34 bartending courses as part of its Alco-\nhol Training Awareness Program (ATAP), which trains licensees and their\nemployees in the appropriate and lawful sales of alcoholic beverages.\n\nBars and restaurant staff can be gatekeepers for victims of human traf-\nficking, and could be the key difference makers in assisting those\nvictims out of their situations.\n\nThis legislation will make it a requirement that a human trafficking\ntraining component be included in all ATAP courses in order to be recog-\nnized by the NYS SLA. This will increase both awareness and understand-\ning of the signs of trafficking and how to report for employees in the\nbar and restaurant industry, to make employees better equipped to prop-\nerly identify and assist victims.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law, provided, however, that the amendments to subdivision 10\nof section 18 of the alcoholic beverage control law made by section one\nof this act shall not affect the expiration of such subdivision and\nshall be deemed to expire therewith; and provided further that section\ntwo of this act shall take effect on the same date as the reversion of\nsubdivision 12 of section 17 of the alcoholic beverage control law as\nprovided in section 4 of chapter 118 of the laws of 2012, as amended.\nEffective immediately, the addition, amendment and/or repeal of any rule\nor regulation necessary for the implementation of this act on its effec-\ntive date are authorized and directed to be made and completed on or\nbefore such effective date.\n",
